#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158408} :activity/id #uuid "d27a6ca7-a613-5fcb-81cc-fb3bce2834e6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049028 :topic/id #uuid "d4a63775-e398-5313-94f3-82976001242f" :topic/math-academy-id 2973} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158527 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we’ll learn another method for solving inhomogeneous linear ODEs: **variation of parameters** (VOP).\n\nRecall that a *first-order linear ODE* can be written in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nTo solve it, we look for a solution of the form\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c(x)$ is the *complementary solution* (solution to the homogeneous equation) and $y_p(x)$ is a *particular solution* (any one solution to the original equation).\n\nIn variation of parameters, we start with the homogeneous solution and then allow its constant to vary with $x.$\n\n- **Step 1:** Solve the corresponding homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nIts general solution has the form\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = {\\color{blue}C}e^{-\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n- **Step 2:** Guess the form of a particular solution by allowing the constant $C$ to depend on $x.$ \n\nWe now choose an **ansatz**, meaning an *assumed form* for a solution. In VOP, we assume\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = {\\color{blue}u(x)}e^{-\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(x)$ is an unknown function.\n\nIntuitively, $y_p(x)$ has the same form as the homogeneous solution $y_c(x),$ but its amplitude changes with $x.$\n\n- **Step 3:** Substitute $y_p(x)$ into the original ODE. \n\nUsing the product rule, we find the derivative:\n\n$$\ny_p' = u' e^{-\\int P\\,\\textrm d x} - u P e^{-\\int P\\,\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nWhen substituted into $y' + Py = Q,$ the second term above cancels with $Py_p,$ leaving\n\n$$\nu'(x)e^{-\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm d x} = Q(x).\n$$\n\nThis allows us to solve for $u'(x),$ integrate to find $u(x),$ and finally recover $y_p(x).$\n\nOn the next slide, we'll construct a suitable VOP ansatz for a particular first-order equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "dc027443-e6e3-5ff7-b9a5-f2b9022b4839" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4691 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0a16aabc-6ee5-5a4a-a2b0-bb3cdc618fdb" :step/math-academy-id 593160 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158409}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158409 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157051 :question/id #uuid "6a492cbf-d378-5193-baf2-c88a2bea63fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21774"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050392}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503705}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"} :step/id #uuid "bbeb54b8-b562-53e6-929d-77208cf333bb" :step/math-academy-id 593161 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158410}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158410 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158462 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + 3y = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nLet's solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nTo find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{5x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the general solution is the sum of $y_c$ and $y_p{:}$\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p = Ae^{-3x} + \\dfrac15 e^{2x}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5fa47c37-0c4f-544e-acd7-42498caa07c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14600 :tutorial/title "Applying Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "08d84b91-6027-5011-a415-d1dd1299ecda" :step/math-academy-id 593164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158411}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158411 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157052 :question/id #uuid "9a26313d-4b93-5d82-98c1-dbecb0bead82" :question/math-academy-id "e-21775"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503742}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "c9a509fa-55ef-5eca-8545-a3c0059a0a95" :step/math-academy-id 593165 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158412}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158463 :tutorial/content "Next, consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = e^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nTo find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant. We call this a **resonant case** because the forcing term is proportional to the homogeneous solution $e^{5x}.$\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\n**Watch out!** Even in resonant cases, the VOP ansatz is unchanged. The only difference is that the resulting equation for $u'$ becomes especially simple. Let's see how.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We omit the constant of integration when solving for $u(x),$ since adding a constant to $u(x)$ would just add a multiple of the homogeneous solution.\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we identify $y_p$ and sum the solutions to find the general solution: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=A{e}^{5x}+x{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2f28a48b-0a76-5234-b54d-0c315719b855" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14601 :tutorial/title "Cases When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "8032045e-3df7-5ea2-a742-40454ef25928" :step/math-academy-id 593168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158413}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157053 :question/id #uuid "bafa3bf1-a867-5259-a6da-226195b45541" :question/math-academy-id "e-21777"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503723}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"} :step/id #uuid "b658e981-ab4e-578e-9e96-6d98a186e23b" :step/math-academy-id 593169}] :activity/title "Variation of Parameters for First-Order Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157522}]}
